steps.instructions:
Add Jedis to `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>redis.clients</groupId>
  <artifactId>jedis</artifactId>
  <version>5.1.0</version>
</dependency>
```

Start `redis-server` locally so there is something to connect to. Create one `JedisPool` per application — it is thread-safe and expensive to build — and borrow a connection per operation:

```java
import redis.clients.jedis.Jedis;
import redis.clients.jedis.JedisPool;

public class ProductCache {
    private final JedisPool pool = new JedisPool("localhost", 6379);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ProductCache app = new ProductCache();
        try (Jedis jedis = app.pool.getResource()) {
            System.out.println(jedis.ping());
        }
    }
}
```

You should see PONG. Call `pool.close()` on shutdown; every later step borrows a `Jedis` from this pool.

Cache-aside puts the application in charge of both stores. Every read checks Redis first; on a miss it loads the source of truth and backfills the cache with a TTL:

```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

public record Product(long id, String name, double price) {}

private static final int CACHE_TTL_SECONDS = 300;
private final ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();

String getProductJson(long id) {
    String key = "product:" + id;
    try (Jedis jedis = pool.getResource()) {
        String cached = jedis.get(key);                // 1. read the cache
        if (cached != null) {
            return cached;                             // 2. hit: Redis served it
        }
        Product product = loadFromDatabase(id);        // 3. miss: read source of truth
        String json = mapper.writeValueAsString(product);
        jedis.setex(key, CACHE_TTL_SECONDS, json);     // 4. backfill, always with a TTL
        return json;
    }
}

// stand-in for your real data layer
Product loadFromDatabase(long id) {
    return new Product(id, "Widget", 9.99);
}
```

`jedis.get` returns null on a miss — that null is the miss signal, no separate EXISTS call needed. The TTL is what makes this a cache instead of a second database: a key nobody refreshes ages out on its own.

On writes, change the source of truth first, then drop the stale cache entry. The next read repopulates it:

```java
void updateProduct(Product updated) {
    saveToDatabase(updated);                    // 1. source of truth first
    try (Jedis jedis = pool.getResource()) {
        jedis.del("product:" + updated.id());   // 2. invalidate, do not update
    }
}
```

Why delete instead of overwrite the cache? A concurrent miss-backfill can interleave with a direct cache write and repopulate stale data that then lives until the TTL. Deletion makes the next read rebuild from the database — and the TTL from step 2 bounds any stale window that slips through anyway.

Cache stampede: a hot key expires while N requests are in flight — all N miss, all hit the database, all backfill the same value. Two standard mitigations:

1. Single-flight lock — only one worker rebuilds while the others retry the cache:

```java
import redis.clients.jedis.params.SetParams;

String winner = jedis.set(key + ":lock", "1",
    SetParams.setParams().nx().ex(10));   // NX: one caller wins; EX: 10s lease
if (winner != null) {
    String fresh = mapper.writeValueAsString(loadFromDatabase(id));
    jedis.setex(key, CACHE_TTL_SECONDS, fresh);
    jedis.del(key + ":lock");
}  // losers: sleep briefly, then re-read the key
```

2. TTL jitter (step 5) — defuses the bulk variant where thousands of keys written in the same batch expire in the same instant.

Add a short comment to your code naming which mitigation fits your workload's write rate, then release resources with `pool.close()`. For the exam, know the vocabulary: single-flight locking with NX+EX, TTL jitter, and stale-while-revalidate patterns. For production, add lock leases short enough to self-heal a crashed holder.
